<header><p class="st-kicker">theocracy · South America</p><h1 class="st-title">Tiwanaku</h1><p class="st-meta">500 CE – 1000 CE (approx.) · capital Tiwanaku</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A highland civilisation near Lake Titicaca whose monumental city and religious art dominated the southern Andes for half a millennium before the Incas.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Tiwanaku began as a village south of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lake-titicaca">Lake Titicaca</a> in present-day <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/republic-of-bolivia">Bolivia</a> in the last centuries BCE and grew into a major ceremonial city by about 500 CE, filling the space left by <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/pukara">Pukará</a>. At 3,850 metres, it is one of the highest cities ever built.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Tiwanaku was ruled by an elite closely tied to religion, centred on monuments such as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/akapana">Akapana</a> pyramid, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kalasasaya">Kalasasaya</a> platform with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gateway-of-the-sun">Gateway of the Sun</a>, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/semi-subterranean-temple">Semi-Subterranean Temple</a>. Raised-field farming by the lake, llama caravans and colonies in warmer valleys such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moquegua">Moquegua</a> supplied the city. Its Staff God imagery spread across the southern <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/andecavi">Andes</a>. At its height the city may have held 20,000 people or more.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Tiwanaku collapsed around 1000 to 1100, probably after a long drought undermined lake-shore agriculture. The region broke up into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aymara">Aymara</a> kingdoms such as the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/lupaca-kingdom">Lupaca Kingdom</a> and <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/colla-kingdom">Colla Kingdom</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/inca-empire">Incas</a> later regarded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lake-titicaca">Titicaca</a> as their place of origin. Tiwanaku became a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco-world-heritage-site">UNESCO World Heritage Site</a> in 2000.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/tiwanaku.jpg" alt="Tiwanaku" width="320" height="256"><figcaption>Image: CLAUDIOLD · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:PUERTA_DEL_SOL_TIWANAKU.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>500 CE – 1000 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>theocracy</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>theocratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Tiwanaku</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Staff God cult</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Possibly Puquina</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd>Bolivia, Peru, Chile</dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Tiahuanaco, Tiwanaku state</dd></dl></aside></div>
